{
  "title": "Websoft9 \u2014 marketplace (2,117\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Websoft9\nDomain: marketplace | Scale: medium | Stars: 2,117\nProblem: Self-hosting and managing multiple open source web applications on a single Linux server is complex and requires deep technical expertise. Websoft9 provides a web-based PaaS/Linux panel that lets users deploy and manage 200+ open source applications without configuration or advanced DevOps knowledge.\nTarget users: Individual developers and hobbyists self-hosting applications, Small to medium businesses running internal tools, DevOps engineers managing single-server deployments, Cloud marketplace customers on AWS, Azure, Alibaba Cloud, Huawei Cloud\nCore features: One-click installation of 200+ open source application templates; Docker Compose GUI for container lifecycle management; Nginx GUI with reverse proxy and free SSL via Let's Encrypt; Web-based Linux system management (network, firewall, storage, systemd, logs); Web-based file browser for server file management; Web-based terminal (remote shell in browser); User account management; Multi-language support (English, Chinese)\nIntegrations: Docker, Docker Compose, Kubernetes, Docker Swarm, Cockpit Project, Portainer, Let's Encrypt, Nginx, AWS Marketplace, Azure Marketplace, Alibaba Cloud Marketplace, Huawei Cloud Marketplace\nTech stack: JavaScript/HTML (Cockpit-based web UI) + Python, Shell, Go + Docker, Docker Compose, Kubernetes, Linux (systemd)\nKey libraries: Cockpit, Portainer, Nginx\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Reuses existing popular technology components (Cockpit, Portainer, Docker Compose) rather than building new stacks, lowering the learning curve for contributors and users.\n- Advocates for microservices-on-a-single-machine approach, challenging the assumption that containerized apps always require clusters or Kubernetes for most workloads.\n- Shell scripting is a first-class citizen alongside Python, reflecting a sysadmin-oriented design philosophy for Linux system integration.\n- Multi-cloud marketplace distribution (AWS, Azure, Alibaba, Huawei) is a core go-to-market strategy, suggesting certified images and reproducible installation scripts are critical infrastructure.",
